"""Phase 2.1 web batch D: extra short/medium questions in varied styles (error messages, review requests, beginner and advanced)."""
from gen21_common import *

B = Bank("programming", "p21wd")
d = B.d

d("web_basics", "whats the difference between GET and POST", "GET asks for a resource and should have no side effects; its parameters travel in the URL, so it can be cached, bookmarked and repeated safely. POST sends data in the request body to create something or trigger an action, and is not cached or repeated automatically. Never put secrets in a GET URL, since URLs are logged by servers and proxies.", diff="easy")
d("web_basics", "Explain what happens when I type a URL into the browser.", "In outline: the browser checks its caches, resolves the domain name to an IP address through DNS, opens a TCP connection (and a TLS handshake for HTTPS), sends an HTTP request, and the server responds with HTML. The browser parses the HTML, fetches linked CSS, JavaScript and images (each possibly a new request), builds the DOM and render tree, runs scripts, and paints the page. Each step has caching and optimisation opportunities, such as CDNs, keep-alive connections and HTTP/2.", diff="medium")
d("web_basics", "Why do I get 'Mixed Content' warnings?", "Your page is loaded over HTTPS but requests a resource (script, image, API) over plain HTTP. Browsers block or warn about this because an attacker could tamper with the insecure resource. Fix it by using `https://` URLs (or protocol-relative paths) for every resource and API call, and make sure your server redirects HTTP to HTTPS.", diff="easy")
d("web_basics", "What is the difference between localStorage, sessionStorage and cookies?", "`localStorage` keeps strings per origin until cleared, about 5 MB, and is not sent to the server. `sessionStorage` is the same but lasts only for the tab session. Cookies are small (about 4 KB), can be sent with every request to the server, can have an expiry and flags like `HttpOnly`, `Secure` and `SameSite`. Use cookies for server sessions, the web storage APIs for non-sensitive client preferences, and none of them for secrets that script shouldn't read.", diff="medium")
d("web_basics", "What does a 304 status mean?", "Not Modified. The browser sent a conditional request (with `If-None-Match` and an `ETag`, or `If-Modified-Since`), and the server says the cached copy is still valid, so it sends no body. It saves bandwidth while keeping content fresh.", diff="medium")
d("web_basics", "What is a CDN and when do I need one?", "A content delivery network caches copies of your static files (and sometimes pages) on servers close to users, which reduces latency and load on your origin. It's valuable when you have users spread over regions, large assets or traffic spikes. For a small site with a local audience, a CDN isn't essential, though many hosts include one anyway.", diff="easy")
d("web_basics", "What is the difference between a framework and a library?", "You call a library: it provides functions you use where you decide. A framework calls you: it defines the structure and flow, and you plug your code into it (routes, components, handlers). Frameworks give conventions and speed but make you work their way; libraries are more flexible but leave more decisions to you.", diff="easy")
d("web_basics", "Why does my fetch not send cookies to the API?", "By default `fetch` doesn't include credentials for cross-origin requests. Set `credentials: 'include'` on the request, and on the server respond with `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` and a specific allowed origin (not `*`). For same-origin requests `credentials: 'same-origin'` (the default) is enough. Cookies with `SameSite=Strict` or `Lax` may also be withheld on cross-site requests.", diff="hard")
d("web_basics", "What is the DOM?", "The Document Object Model is the browser's in-memory tree of objects representing your HTML page. JavaScript uses it to read and change the page: `document.querySelector('#title').textContent = 'Hi'`. Changing the DOM triggers layout and painting, so batch updates and avoid reading layout properties in the middle of writing them in a loop.", diff="easy")
d("web_basics", "How does the browser decide when to use a cached file?", "Through HTTP caching headers. `Cache-Control: max-age=31536000, immutable` lets the browser reuse a file without asking for a year, which is ideal for fingerprinted assets. `Cache-Control: no-cache` means \"revalidate before using\", typically with an `ETag`. Use long caching with hashed file names for JS/CSS/images, and short or revalidated caching for HTML so users get new versions.", diff="medium")
d("css_debug", "My `position: sticky` header doesn't stick.", "Sticky positioning needs three things: a `top` (or other offset) value, no ancestor with `overflow: hidden`, `auto` or `scroll` between the element and the scrolling container (those make that ancestor the scroll context), and enough room in its parent, since it only sticks within the parent's box. Check the ancestors in dev tools for `overflow` and for a parent that is only as tall as the header itself.", diff="hard")
d("css_debug", "Why is there a gap under my image?", "Images are inline elements by default and sit on the text baseline, which leaves space for descenders below them. Set `img { display: block; }` or `vertical-align: top`. Also check there is no line-height or font-size on the container creating extra space.", diff="medium")
d("css_debug", "What does `rem` vs `em` vs `px` mean?", "`px` is a fixed CSS pixel. `rem` is relative to the root element's font size (usually 16px), so it scales with the user's browser setting and gives consistent spacing. `em` is relative to the current element's font size, which compounds when nested. Use `rem` for most sizing so the layout respects user preferences, `em` for things that should scale with their own text (such as button padding), and `px` for thin borders.", diff="easy")
d("css_debug", "My animation is janky. How can I make it smoother?", "Animate properties the browser can handle on the compositor: `transform` and `opacity`. Animating `width`, `height`, `top` or `left` triggers layout on every frame. Avoid layout-triggering work during the animation, use `will-change` sparingly, and respect users who prefer less motion:\n\n```css\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  * { animation: none !important; transition: none !important; }\n}\n```", diff="hard")
d("node_extra", "How do I stop a Node app from logging my users' emails and tokens?", "Treat logs as data that spreads. Log events and ids rather than payloads, redact known sensitive keys (password, token, authorization, cookie) in a central logger wrapper, and never log full request headers or bodies by default. Review error handlers, since exceptions often include the data that triggered them. Set retention limits on log storage and restrict who can read it.", diff="medium")
d("node_extra", "What's the event loop in one paragraph?", "Node runs your JavaScript on one thread. When you start asynchronous work (a timer, a network or file operation), Node hands it to the system and keeps going; when it finishes, its callback is queued. The event loop picks queued callbacks and runs them one at a time, with promise callbacks (microtasks) running before the next timer or I/O callback. Long synchronous code delays everything, because nothing else can run until it returns.", diff="medium")
d("node_extra", "What should a good `package.json` scripts section contain?", "Short, consistent commands for the workflows people repeat: `dev` (run with reload), `build`, `start` (production), `test`, `lint` and `format`. CI should call the same scripts developers use. Commit the lockfile so installs are reproducible, and pin the Node version with the `engines` field or an `.nvmrc` file.", diff="easy")
d("python_web", "Flask or FastAPI for a new small API?", "Both are fine. FastAPI gives you request validation, automatic OpenAPI documentation and first-class async support through type hints, so it suits JSON APIs. Flask is minimal and flexible, with a huge ecosystem, and works well for server-rendered pages or when you want complete control. If the API's main job is validating and serving JSON, FastAPI usually saves time.", diff="easy")
d("python_web", "My Python API leaks memory over days. How do I find the cause?", "Start by confirming it: graph the process's memory over time. Then look for common causes: unbounded caches or global dictionaries/lists that only grow, objects kept alive by closures or reference cycles with `__del__`, unclosed connections or file handles, and per-request data stored at module level. `tracemalloc` (standard library) can compare snapshots to show which lines allocate memory that isn't freed. As a mitigation, restart workers periodically (for example the max-requests setting in your server), but still fix the root cause.", diff="hard")
d("python_web", "How do I run tasks on a schedule in a Python web app?", "Don't use a sleeping thread inside the web process: with several workers it runs multiple times and dies with the process. Use a separate scheduler (cron, a systemd timer, or your platform's scheduled jobs) to run a command, or a job queue with a scheduler component. Make jobs idempotent and log each run, so a double run or a missed run doesn't cause damage.", diff="medium")
d("python_web", "Review my Dockerfile:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM python:latest\nCOPY . .\nRUN pip install -r requirements.txt\nCMD python app.py\n```", "It works, but several improvements matter:\n\n- Pin the base image (`python:3.12-slim`) instead of `latest`, so rebuilds are reproducible.\n- Copy `requirements.txt` and install before copying the rest, so the dependency layer is cached when only code changes.\n- Set a `WORKDIR`, avoid running as root, and add a `.dockerignore` (exclude `.git`, `.env`, caches).\n- Use the exec form for `CMD`.\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM python:3.12-slim\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY requirements.txt .\nRUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\nCOPY . .\nRUN useradd -m appuser\nUSER appuser\nCMD [\"python\", \"app.py\"]\n```", diff="medium")
d("python_web", "pydantic gives me 'Field required' for a field that I think I sent. Why?", "The usual causes: the key name doesn't match exactly (case, `userId` vs `user_id`), the value is nested under another key, the client sent the body as form data or with the wrong `Content-Type` so the body wasn't parsed as JSON, or you declared the model as a query parameter. Read the error's `loc` list, which shows where Pydantic looked, and print the raw request body to compare. If you need to accept a different key name, use an alias: `user_id: int = Field(alias='userId')`.", diff="medium")
d("ts_extra", "What does `readonly` do in TypeScript and does it freeze the object?", "`readonly` prevents assignment to a property or array through that type at compile time. It does not freeze the object at runtime; other code with a non-readonly reference can still change it, and `Object.freeze` is what makes a shallow runtime freeze. Use `readonly` in function parameters to document and enforce that you won't mutate the argument.", diff="medium")
d("ts_extra", "How do I type the result of an API call in TypeScript safely?", "Define the type you expect, then validate the data against it before trusting it. A hand-written guard works for small shapes:\n\n```typescript\ninterface User { id: number; name: string }\n\nfunction isUser(x: unknown): x is User {\n  return typeof x === 'object' && x !== null\n    && typeof (x as any).id === 'number'\n    && typeof (x as any).name === 'string';\n}\n\nconst data: unknown = await res.json();\nif (!isUser(data)) throw new Error('Unexpected response shape');\n```\n\nFor larger APIs, generate types and validators from an OpenAPI spec or a schema library, so the checks and types come from one source.", diff="hard")
d("git_extra", "How do I undo `git add` for a file I staged by mistake?", "`git restore --staged path/to/file` unstages it and keeps your edits in the working tree. On older Git versions, `git reset HEAD path/to/file` does the same. To see the state afterwards, run `git status`.", diff="easy")
d("git_extra", "What is the difference between `git revert` and `git reset`?", "`git reset` moves the branch pointer back, discarding commits from the branch history (and with `--hard`, your working changes too), which rewrites history. `git revert` adds a new commit that undoes an earlier one, leaving history intact. Revert is the right choice for commits already shared; reset is for local clean-up of work nobody else has.", diff="medium")
d("git_extra", "What does a `.gitignore` do and what should go in it?", "It lists patterns of files Git should not track. Typically: dependency folders (`node_modules/`, `.venv/`), build output (`dist/`, `build/`), caches and logs, editor settings, operating-system files and, above all, local secrets such as `.env`. It affects only untracked files, so something already committed must be removed with `git rm --cached` first.", diff="easy")
d("review_snippet", "Can you review this Express route?\n\n```javascript\napp.get('/files', (req, res) => {\n  const name = req.query.name;\n  res.sendFile('/srv/data/' + name);\n});\n```", "This is a path traversal vulnerability: a request like `?name=../../etc/passwd` escapes `/srv/data`. Also nothing checks that `name` exists or is a string. Resolve the path and check it stays inside the base directory, or use `sendFile`'s `root` option, and validate the name:\n\n```javascript\nconst path = require('node:path');\n\napp.get('/files', (req, res) => {\n  const name = String(req.query.name || '');\n  if (!/^[\\w.-]+$/.test(name)) return res.status(400).json({ error: 'bad_name' });\n  res.sendFile(name, { root: '/srv/data' }, (err) => {\n    if (err) res.status(404).json({ error: 'not_found' });\n  });\n});\n```\n\nAn allow-list of file names is even safer if the set is known.", diff="hard")
d("review_snippet", "Look at this and tell me what could go wrong in production:\n\n```python\ndef charge(user_id, amount):\n    balance = get_balance(user_id)\n    if balance >= amount:\n        set_balance(user_id, balance - amount)\n        return True\n    return False\n```", "It has a race condition: two concurrent calls can both read the same balance, both pass the check, and both write, so the user is charged less than they should be (or goes negative). Make the check-and-update atomic, for example in one SQL statement inside a transaction:\n\n```sql\nUPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - :amount\nWHERE id = :user_id AND balance >= :amount;\n```\n\nThen check the affected row count (1 means success). Also use integer cents or `Decimal` for money, validate that `amount` is positive, and make the operation idempotent with a request key so a retry doesn't charge twice.", diff="hard")
d("review_snippet", "Is there a problem with this retry logic?\n\n```javascript\nasync function getWithRetry(url) {\n  while (true) {\n    try {\n      return await fetch(url);\n    } catch (e) {}\n  }\n}\n```", "Yes: it retries forever, with no delay and no limit, and swallows every error. If the service is down it spins in a hot loop, flooding it (and your logs are silent). Bound the attempts, back off between tries, and surface the final error:\n\n```javascript\nasync function getWithRetry(url, tries = 4) {\n  for (let i = 0; i < tries; i++) {\n    try {\n      const res = await fetch(url);\n      if (res.status < 500) return res;   // don't retry client errors\n    } catch (e) {\n      if (i === tries - 1) throw e;\n    }\n    await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 200 * 2 ** i));\n  }\n  throw new Error('service unavailable after retries');\n}\n```\n\nOnly retry operations that are safe to repeat.", diff="hard")
d("review_snippet", "Is this a good way to build a URL?\n\n```javascript\nconst url = 'https://api.example.com/search?q=' + userInput + '&limit=10';\n```", "Not if `userInput` is arbitrary: characters like `&`, `#`, `=` or spaces change the meaning of the URL (parameter injection) or break it. Build URLs with the `URL` and `URLSearchParams` classes, which encode values for you:\n\n```javascript\nconst url = new URL('https://api.example.com/search');\nurl.searchParams.set('q', userInput);\nurl.searchParams.set('limit', '10');\n```\n\nUse `encodeURIComponent` if you must build a string manually.", diff="medium")
d("review_snippet", "Review this CSS:\n\n```css\n.btn { width: 100px; height: 30px; font-size: 11px; color: #aaa; background: #eee; }\n.btn:focus { outline: none; }\n```", "Accessibility and robustness issues:\n\n- `outline: none` removes the keyboard focus indicator; keep one or replace it with a visible style (for example with `:focus-visible`).\n- `#aaa` on `#eee` has very low contrast (well under the 4.5:1 WCAG guideline for normal text).\n- 11px text is small, and fixed `width`/`height` break when the text grows or the user increases font size; use padding and `min-height`, with `rem` units.\n\n```css\n.btn { padding: 0.5rem 1rem; min-height: 2.5rem; font-size: 1rem; color: #1a1a1a; background: #e6e6e6; }\n.btn:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid #2457d6; outline-offset: 2px; }\n```", diff="medium")
d("review_snippet", "I wrote this SQL in my API. Opinions?\n\n```python\nsql = \"UPDATE users SET name = '%s' WHERE id = %s\" % (name, user_id)\ncur.execute(sql)\n```", "This is vulnerable to SQL injection: `name` or `user_id` can contain quotes or SQL and change the statement. Never use string formatting for queries. Pass parameters separately:\n\n```python\ncur.execute(\"UPDATE users SET name = %s WHERE id = %s\", (name, user_id))\n```\n\nThe driver quotes the values safely (the `%s` here is the driver's placeholder, not Python formatting). Also check that the current user is allowed to update that `user_id`.", diff="medium")

